  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nEarly Tuesday afternoon, mostly sunny skies and temperatures in the \nlow 70s were observed across central Kentucky and southern Indiana. \nWater vapor satellite showed broad west-northwesterly flow over the \nOhio Valley/Midwest, while a mid level disturbance was diving \nsoutheast across the Rockies and into the High Plains. It is this \nfeature that will intensify as it phases with a northern stream jet \nover our neck of the woods tomorrow night into Thursday, helping to \ndeepen a surface low as it shifts eastward into the Mid-Atlantic.\n\n*** SEVERE WEATHER(?) TOMORROW EVENING ***\n\nAhead of the aforementioned low, the area will see a brief push of \nsouthwesterly warm advection and surge of low level moisture with \ndewpoints climbing into the mid to upper 50s tomorrow afternoon and \nevening. At the same time, mid-level lapse rates will modestly \nsteepen to favor a few hundred J/kg MLCAPE, which, coupled with 35-\n45 kt 0-6km layer shear, will at least favor a few thunderstorms and \nperhaps localized small hail tomorrow evening. SPC has the area \noutlooked with a level 1 of 5 (marginal) risk for severe weather, \nand we can't dismiss it given the impressive kinematics. However, we \nthink the overall risk for severe weather is quite low given the \npoor low-level lapse rates (which limits the wind and tornado risk). \nWe'll keep an eye on storms tomorrow evening in case a cell or two \ngets feisty, though. Despite medium-high (60-70%) confidence in rain \nalong the developing cold front tomorrow evening, which is forecast \nto move across the area from west to east between roughly 4 and 11pm \nEST, rainfall amounts should remain meager given lack of deep layer \nmoisture and fast storm motion (40-50mph).\n\n*** BREEZY AND SHARPLY COOLER BEGINNING WEDNESDAY NIGHT ***\n\nBehind the cold front, rapid pressure rises behind the deepening low \nshould promote an uptick in winds from the northwest. Nocturnal \ntiming should offset the magnitude of the winds, but still we \nsuspect we'll have sustained 10-20 mph winds with sporadic gusts up \nto 30 mph tomorrow night into Thursday - ushering a much cooler \nairmass into the region, with LREF mean bringing 850mb temps into \nthe -4 to -5 degC range by Thursday night/Friday morning. \nConsequently, Thursday will be a breezy and seasonably chilly day \nwith highs in the low-mid 50s. In addition, there may be just enough \nwrap around moisture to give us a few rain showers during the \nafternoon when surface heating promotes steep low level lapse rates \nand marginal instability.\n\n*** CHILLY THIS WEEKEND, SLOWLY MODERATING NEXT WEEK ***\n\nFriday will likely be the coolest day in the forecast period with \nNBM giving us only a 20% chance high temperatures climb out of the \n40s during the afternoon. However, cool weather will continue \nthrough the upcoming weekend as the upper level low lingers across \nthe Great Lakes/Northeast, with Saturday and Sunday forecast highs \nin the upper 40s to low 50s and overnight lows solidly in the 20s. \nConditions will gradually moderate next work week as ridging \nattempts to rebuild across the nation's midsection.",
